From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: no Dock, menu bar or hot corner from inside full screen

What: the other displays' full-screen windows ask macOS for the same
presentation as the main window (Dock and menu bar hidden, not auto-hidden),
and while the VM has the pointer in full screen the Mac's cursor never
reaches a corner of its display or the Dock's edge: within 200 points of
them it is detached from the mouse and stays where it is, and the guest's
pointer moves on by the mouse's motion (each event's delta: macOS's own
motion after its acceleration, in points). The guest's pointer reaches the
edges and corners; the Mac's cursor is kept at least 3 points off them.
Edges shared with another display stay open. The maths is in
ui/omacvm-pointer-guard.h (omacvm-cocoa-pointer-guard.patch). The cursor
is given back where the guest's pointer is when it leaves the zone or our
windows, on leaving full screen, on a Space change, on ungrab and on quit.
The Dock's side is read again on each full screen. All of it follows the
immersive option (on by default; immersive=off keeps macOS's behaviour; the
app's setting "Keep the Dock and hot corners away in full screen").

Why: in the user's 2.8.0/2.9.0 RC test the Dock came up at the edge of the
external display (its full-screen Space used macOS's default auto-hide), and
a hot corner set to "put display to sleep" fired from inside the full-screen
VM. The presentation option alone did not keep the Dock away on the other
display (tried), and moving the cursor back after it reached a corner did
not stop the hot corner (tried: the display went to sleep), so the cursor
must never get there.
2.8.0 to 2.9.0 RC4 warped the detached cursor along on every event. A warp
comes back in the next event's delta (SDL and GLFW correct for this on
macOS), so each delta carried all the earlier ones and the pointer raced
near corners and the Dock's edge (user, 2026-10-05: "at the corners it 20x
the acceleration"; the test's model of that code: the 20th step is 19 times
the motion). Now the detached cursor is not moved; the one warp left (off
an outer edge, when the cursor got onto it in one go) is taken out of the
next delta. AppKit can hand one event to a view twice; it counts once.
Known limit: a flick that lands on the corner in one event has reached it
before the guard sees the event.

Upstream: not applicable (OmacVM's display windows; QEMU has one window).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/display/test-pointer-guard.c (motion in the centre,
near a corner, along the Dock's edge, across the zone's border and a shared
display edge, the same event twice, a flick; run at build time and in CI).
By hand: full screen on the MacBook and an external display, mouse and
trackpad: the pointer moves the same everywhere; pushed into each corner and
along each edge: no Dock, no menu bar, no hot corner (notes:
pointer-corners track).

